WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.732
Use this final guide when you are unsure.

00:00:02.732 --> 00:00:06.487
Ask what the outer query needs from the inner query.

00:00:06.487 --> 00:00:09.122
One value suggests a scalar.

00:00:09.122 --> 00:00:16.272
Membership suggests IN. A related-row question suggests EXISTS or NOT EXISTS.

00:00:16.272 --> 00:00:19.022
A comparison against a set suggests ANY or ALL.

00:00:19.022 --> 00:00:23.607
Intermediate rows suggest a derived table.

00:00:23.607 --> 00:00:28.082
Then ask whether the inner query depends on the current outer row.

00:00:28.082 --> 00:00:30.667
If yes, it is correlated.

00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:36.712
Connect this idea to a hospital identifying patients above a benchmark or with no follow-up.

00:00:36.712 --> 00:00:42.372
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:42.372 --> 00:00:50.167
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:55.712
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:55.712 --> 00:01:01.600
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
